<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gobarau Minaret</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Gobarau Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gobarau Minaret is a tall <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> tower in Katsina, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, the remnant of a mosque traditionally dated to the late 15th or early 16th century. Tradition links it to the first Muslim king of Katsina, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-korau">Muhammadu Korau</a>. The mosque served as a centre of Islamic teaching that drew scholars from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gobarau-minaret.jpg" alt="Gobarau Minaret" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derric Evans · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gobarau_Minaret_Katsina.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The minaret, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> as Hasumiyya and also spelled Gobirau or Goborau, stands about 15 metres tall in the centre of Katsina, the capital of Katsina State. It is an early example of Hausa Muslim architecture. The founding of the mosque is credited to the influence of the scholar Sheikh Muhammad Abdulkarim Almaghili, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, who taught in Katsina when he visited the town late in the 15th century, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-korau">Muhammadu Korau</a>. The building was modelled on the mosques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> of that time.</p><p>A popular story tells that when Korau, having killed Jibda-Yaki Sanau, the last pagan king of Katsina, chose the site, his scholars disagreed with a stranger, Mallam Jodoma, over the direction of the qibla, until Jodoma pointed his staff and the Ka'abah appeared. Korau made him chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, but jealous rivals persuaded the king that Jodoma wanted the throne, and he was banished to Guga, where he died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The mosque was built as a centre of spiritual and intellectual life and was later used as a madrassa. By the beginning of the 16th century Katsina had become an important commercial and academic centre of Hausaland, and the Gobarau Mosque had grown into a well-known institution of higher Islamic education. According to the same popular tradition, the tower also served as a lookout for invading armies.</p><p>It remained Katsina's central mosque until early in the 19th century, when Sarkin Katsina Ummarun Dallaje, who ruled from 1805 to 1835, built a new mosque. That building was later demolished by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-dikko">Muhammadu Dikko</a>, ruler from 1906 to 1944, who built the Katsina Grand Central Mosque, the Masallacin Dutsi, still in use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In a city known as a centre of theology, the tower has become a symbol of Katsina. The former mosque and its minaret were renovated under Sarkin Katsina Muhammadu Kabir Usman, who reigned from 1981 to 2008. The minaret is visited by tourists, together with the Kusugu well in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daura">Daura</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gobarau Minaret” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Katsina stood at the southern end of a major trans-Saharan route through Agadez and became a centre of trade in cloth, leather, slaves and kola, and of Islamic learning, with scholars teaching at the <b>Gobarau</b> mosque.</blockquote><a href="/state/katsina-kingdom">Katsina Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
